content.content:
The distributions differ and should. Compiled code is mostly small leaf functions
that neither branch nor merge, which is the null reading, while a protein kept by
selection is under pressure to come back. The alphabet, the pairing and the rule do
not differ by a symbol.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=1cm,y=1cm,font=\footnotesize]
  % The bars are proportions, computed here from the raw counts, because the two
  % corpora differ in size by an order and only the shape is comparable.
  \def\barw{12.2}
  \newcommand{\segment}[5]{%
    \draw[draw=white,line width=0.7pt,fill=#1!30] (#2,#4) rectangle (#3,#4+0.84);
    \pgfmathsetmacro{\segw}{#3-#2}%
    \ifdim\segw cm>0.62cm
      \node[font=\scriptsize] at ({(#2+#3)/2},#4+0.42) {#5};
    \else
      \node[font=\scriptsize,anchor=south] at ({(#2+#3)/2},#4+0.94) {#5};
      \draw[black!45,thin] ({(#2+#3)/2},#4+0.84) -- ({(#2+#3)/2},#4+0.94);
    \fi}
  \newcommand{\sweeprow}[6]{%
    \pgfmathsetmacro{\tot}{#3+#4+#5+#6}%
    \node[anchor=west,align=left,font=\small] at (0,#1+1.22) {#2};
    \segment{cWork}{0}{\barw*#3/\tot}{#1}{#3}%
    \segment{cFuse}{\barw*#3/\tot}{\barw*(#3+#4)/\tot}{#1}{#4}%
    \segment{cIdle}{\barw*(#3+#4)/\tot}{\barw*(#3+#4+#5)/\tot}{#1}{#5}%
    \segment{cIll}{\barw*(#3+#4+#5)/\tot}{\barw}{#1}{#6}%
    \draw[black!45] (0,#1) rectangle (\barw,#1+0.84);}
  \sweeprow{0}{Crystallographic models of proteins}{9}{22}{13}{21}
  \sweeprow{-2.5}{A stock system executable}{8}{261}{314}{1}
  \foreach \lab/\col [count=\j from 0] in
    {closes/cWork,{hangs open}/cFuse,null/cIdle,{ill-typed}/cIll}{%
    \draw[draw=black!45,fill=\col!30] (3.05*\j,-3.65) rectangle (3.05*\j+0.42,-3.35);
    \node[anchor=west,font=\scriptsize] at (3.05*\j+0.55,-3.5) {\lab};}
\end{tikzpicture}
\caption[One rule over two texts]{The same alphabet, the same pairing and the same rule,
over deposited protein structures and over a compiled executable, shown as proportions
because the two corpora are nothing like the same size. The distributions differ and
should: compiled code is mostly small leaf functions that neither branch nor merge,
which is the null reading, while a protein kept by selection is under pressure to come
back. Not one symbol of the rule differs between the two rows, which is the whole of
the join, and it says the genetic code was never the subject.}
\label{fig:sweep}
\end{figure}


That is the join, and it is larger than the parts that fed it. The letters and the
rule were worked out to read the genetic code, and they read machine code with no
adjustment, which means the genetic code was never the subject. Life is not a special
kind of matter that acquired information. It is one of the things this alphabet reads,
and the reason biology looked like a different order of problem is that nobody had the
alphabet.

