215:\caption{The $2$-adic filtration at $d=2048$, closed at $k=15$. Above: valuations of cyclic invariants of $\Cl_{\pt^k}$. The tail freezes from $k=5$; the leading pair advances as a rigid body until meeting the ceiling $v_2(2d)=12$. Below: the gap $a-b$, pinned at the tail's valuation through the fourfold regime, collapsing once the ceiling is reached. The predicted exponent $k=12$ is the corner.}
219:The calibration and prediction are recorded in \Lean{} alongside tower data. The tower has been carried to $k=15$: every ray class degree is computed, not extrapolated, and the halving sustains at exactly two for $k=12,13,14,15$.
392:\begin{figure}[H]
394:\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{figs/horn_side.pdf}
395:\caption{Side-on section in the syzygy axis plane ($xz$, $y=0$). The two lobes are the horn tube's cross-section, centred at $x=\pm R$ with radius $R$: they are tangent at the origin, and that tangency is the pinch $\odot$. The $\in$ split shell $\lambda_C$ is the dashed circle centred on $\odot$ with radius $r=R/2$; the evaluator sphere has the same radius and centres one radius along the axis, so it passes through $\odot$ and through $\ni$ at $x=2$. The heavy segment between them is the coupler. The evaluator trine is $\top$, $\bot$, $\boxplus$ at $x=1$ and $z=0,\pm\sqrt3/2$: collinear in this section, hence edge-on, standing at $120^\circ$ only in the perpendicular plane. The marker at $x=2R$ is $\gamma(0)$, the horn-torus equatorial curve at $t=0$: the outer equator. The horn is $\gamma(\pi)$, the pinch at the origin where the two lobes meet, which is $\odot$ and is labelled as such.}
401:\begin{figure}[H]
403:\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{figs/horn_equator.pdf}
404:\caption{Top-down section at $R=r$ ($xy$, $z=0$). The pinch $\odot$ sits at origin where the hole closes; $\lambda_C$ and the evaluator sphere are the two dashed circles, $\ni$ sits at $x=2$, and $\gamma(0)$ likewise on the outer equator. The horn is $\gamma(\pi)$, the pinch at the origin, marked there. The three points at $120^\circ$ on that equator are the $A_2$ ROOTS, $n_+ = +1$ against two $n_-$, and their triangle is the $(16,3)$ knot's three poloidal windings read as circuit. The evaluator trine is NOT that triangle: in this projection it collapses to the short segment at $x=1$, which is the same trine drawn edge-on in Figure~\ref{fig:cfg} seen now along its own axis. Rendered from the kernel geometry generator, print theme.}
410:The IMASM machine's native alphabet is B4: four values $\{N,T,F,B\}$, two bits each. A byte is four B4 cells; the \Sixteen{} carrier is one layer up. Input, computation, and output live in the same alphabet. The machine reads itself because there is no outside alphabet to translate through.

