$10$ & $8388608$   & $23$ & $[2048, 256, 8, 2]$ \\
$11$ & $33554432$  & $25$ & $[4096, 512, 8, 2]$ \\
$12$ & $67108864$  & $26$ & $[4096, 1024, 8, 2]$ \\
$13$ & $134217728$ & $27$ & $[4096, 2048, 8, 2]$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

Read in valuations, every level from $k=5$ upward is $[a,b,3,1]$: the tail is frozen. The gap $a-b=3$ equals the third invariant's valuation and holds throughout the fourfold regime. Growth of four is one invariant advancing while its distance to the next is pinned. The regime changes when the leader saturates at $12 = v_2(2d)$: a ceiling, not a coincidence. The leading invariant cannot exceed the $2$-adic content of $2d$. Once saturated, only the second can move; the gap collapses through $2,1,0$ at $k=12,13,14$; at $k=15$ the leader resumes.

\Cref{fig:filtration} shows this geometry: a staircase with a ceiling. The predicted exponent is the corner—the last level where the gap is pinned, the last moment before the structure changes character. An error of one either way is a factor of two or four in degree.

\begin{figure}[H]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{figs/filtration_2048.pdf}
\caption{The $2$-adic filtration at $d=2048$, closed at $k=15$. Above: valuations of cyclic invariants of $\Cl_{\pt^k}$. The tail freezes from $k=5$; the leading pair advances as a rigid body until meeting the ceiling $v_2(2d)=12$. Below: the gap $a-b$, pinned at the tail's valuation through the fourfold regime, collapsing once the ceiling is reached. The predicted exponent $k=12$ is the corner.}
\label{fig:filtration}
\end{figure}

The calibration and prediction are recorded in \Lean{} alongside tower data. The tower has been carried to $k=15$: every ray class degree is computed, not extrapolated, and the halving sustains at exactly two for $k=12,13,14,15$.

...

Four is the only nontrivial place where enumerating subsets and self-application yield the same count. This licenses reading sixteen as \emph{four self-applied}: the base referring to itself. This fixed point is what makes the trilattice capable of carrying its own conductor.

\subsection{The Geometric Realization: The Horn Torus and $(16,3)$ Knot}

Independently of ray class computations, the evaluator arms trace a $(16,3)$ torus knot on the horn torus: sixteen toroidal windings against three poloidal ones. The bridge number three matches the three arms carrying the four-valued kernel.

\begin{figure}[H]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{figs/horn_side.pdf}
\caption{Side-on section in the syzygy axis plane ($xz$, $y=0$). The two lobes are the horn tube's cross-section, centred at $x=\pm R$ with radius $R$: they are tangent at the origin, and that tangency is the pinch $\odot$. The $\in$ split shell $\lambda_C$ is the dashed circle centred on $\odot$ with radius $r=R/2$; the evaluator sphere has the same radius and centres one radius along the axis, so it passes through $\odot$ and through $\ni$ at $x=2$. The heavy segment between them is the coupler. The evaluator trine is $\top$, $\bot$, $\boxplus$ at $x=1$ and $z=0,\pm\sqrt3/2$: collinear in this section, hence edge-on, standing at $120^\circ$ only in the perpendicular plane. The marker at $x=2R$ is $\gamma(0)$, the horn-torus equatorial curve at $t=0$: the outer equator. The horn is $\gamma(\pi)$, the pinch at the origin where the two lobes meet, which is $\odot$ and is labelled as such.}
\label{fig:cfg}
\end{figure}

The horn torus is the degenerate case $R=r$: tube radius equals centre radius, hole closes to a point. That point is the pinch—where the boundary meets itself. This is not a drawing defect. It is the condition enabling self-reference. The three evaluator arms at $120^\circ$ span the $A_2$ triangle; the three poloidal windings are those arms seen as circuit rather than triple. The sixteen toroidal windings are the trilattice's states carried once around. Nothing is scaled: $R=r$ exactly, lobes touch rather than overlap.

