The horn torus is the degenerate case $R=r$: tube radius equals centre radius, hole closes to a point. That point is the pinch—where the boundary meets itself. This is not a drawing defect. It is the condition enabling self-reference. The three evaluator arms at $120^\circ$ span the $A_2$ triangle; the three poloidal windings are those arms seen as circuit rather than triple. The sixteen toroidal windings are the trilattice's states carried once around. Nothing is scaled: $R=r$ exactly, lobes touch rather than overlap.

\begin{figure}[H]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{figs/horn_equator.pdf}
\caption{Top-down section at $R=r$ ($xy$, $z=0$). The pinch $\odot$ sits at origin where the hole closes; $\lambda_C$ and the evaluator sphere are the two dashed circles, $\ni$ sits at $x=2$, and $\gamma(0)$ likewise on the outer equator. The horn is $\gamma(\pi)$, the pinch at the origin, marked there. The three points at $120^\circ$ on that equator are the $A_2$ ROOTS, $n_+ = +1$ against two $n_-$, and their triangle is the $(16,3)$ knot's three poloidal windings read as circuit. The evaluator trine is NOT that triangle: in this projection it collapses to the short segment at $x=1$, which is the same trine drawn edge-on in Figure~\ref{fig:cfg}; Figure~\ref{fig:axial} takes the view along its own axis, where it stands. Rendered from the kernel geometry generator, print theme.}
\label{fig:horn}
\end{figure}

\Cref{fig:axial} is Figure~\ref{fig:cfg} again with the viewpoint moved a quarter turn round the torus, looking down the syzygy axis instead of across it. The horn is symmetric about that axis, so the outline does not change at all: the same two lobes of radius $R$ centred one radius either side of the pinch, tangent at the origin, and the pinch is $\odot$ where it was. What changes is what the axis does. Across it, $\odot$ at $x=0$, the coupler, and $\ni$ at $x=2$ are spread out and $\lambda_C$ and the evaluator sphere are two circles side by side; down it they stack, so the two circles are concentric and of equal radius and become one circle, and the marks on the axis sit one behind the other with the pinch nearest. And the trine, which is edge-on in both of the other views and collapses there to three points on a line at $x=1$, is face-on here: $\top$, $\bot$, $\boxplus$ at $120^\circ$ on that circle, the equilateral triangle it has been all along.

